---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/email-design';
const title = 'Email design for Shopify campaigns: content, rendering and release checks';
const desc = 'Plan readable Shopify campaign emails with accessible content, safe personalization, consistent offers, rendering fixtures and controlled template releases.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Email design</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Design email around the decision a customer needs to make</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A campaign needs a clear purpose, readable terms and a working destination. Build the message around that contract, then check the actual email with representative content and recipient states before releasing it.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Explore app options</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12 space-y-8">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Write the message contract before drawing the layout</h2>
      <p>Choose one primary job: explain a product, announce availability, present an offer or help someone complete a task. Write the audience, intended next action and reason to send now. Give the subject, preview text, heading and main link the same promise. Preview text should add useful context rather than repeat the subject or expose template instructions.</p>
      <p>Arrange the content in decision order: what is available, why it matters, essential restrictions, then the action. Keep secondary navigation subordinate. A product education email can lead with a use case and link to details; an offer email should put material eligibility and expiry information close to the offer. Do not hide a qualifying minimum spend in artwork or rely on urgency language to explain an ambiguous deadline.</p>
      <p>Use a reusable set of content modules with owners and explicit fields for heading, body, image, image alternative, link label, destination and terms. Prefer fewer modules when there is no additional decision to support. The editorial brief should say what can be omitted if product data is unavailable.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Make text, images and links understandable together</h2>
      <p>Keep essential copy, prices, terms and action labels as live text. Review heading order and reading order with images hidden and with a screen reader in the intended email clients. A layout that looks ordered on a desktop can still read confusingly when its columns are linearized. Use descriptive links such as “View the linen care guide” so customers can distinguish destinations.</p>
      <p>Choose image alternatives by purpose. W3C WAI's <a class="link" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/">image alternative decision tree</a> distinguishes meaningful images, decorative images and functional linked images: describe the relevant meaning, leave purely decorative alternatives empty, and convey the destination or action when an image supplies the link's meaning. This is guidance for HTML image alternatives; it does not certify an email's accessibility or rendering.</p>
      <p>Check text and controls against their actual backgrounds, including image overlays and dark appearances. Give links a visible distinguishing treatment and check keyboard focus where supported. Inspect the plain-text alternative for meaningful order, offer terms, destinations and footer information. A decorative image failing to load should not remove the only explanation of the offer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Design for missing, long and localized content</h2>
      <p>Define a fallback for every personalized field before adding it. A missing first name can use a neutral greeting; a missing product recommendation can omit the module or use approved general content. Never substitute another customer's data. Validate types, escape untrusted text and restrict destinations to approved schemes and hosts. Treat a missing required offer value as a reason to block release rather than display an empty price or raw template token.</p>
      <p>Preview the longest realistic product names, translated headings, large currency values and multiple variants. Check wrapping in buttons and footers, reading direction where relevant, locale-specific currency and dates, and explicit deadline timezones. Have a reviewer who understands the language approve the meaning of translated terms. Do not shorten a restriction merely to fit the design.</p>
      <p>Specify which values are frozen at approval and which are resolved at send time. For dynamic prices or availability, record the data source and freshness expectation. If a required feed is stale or fails, pause the affected send or use an approved non-offer fallback. Keep customer-specific preview data synthetic and remove obsolete test exports according to the team's retention rules.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Check the sent artifact on mobile and in relevant clients</h2>
      <p>Use a client matrix informed by the audience and support history: include relevant mobile and desktop mail applications, webmail, light and dark appearances, and images enabled and disabled. Record application versions and test dates. Expand the matrix when a material audience segment or new defect warrants it; there is no universal width, file-size threshold or client list that proves every recipient will see the same result.</p>
      <p>Inspect narrow screens, enlarged text, stacked columns, tap spacing, horizontal overflow, cropped imagery, button labels and the full footer. In dark appearances, check logos, transparent assets, background changes and text visibility. Send a real test through the intended delivery path: editor previews alone do not exercise tracking redirects, final template substitution or every client transformation.</p>
      <p>Check the final message source and visible tail for missing content, clipping or unwanted expansion from repeated modules. Keep assets purposeful and inspect their loading behavior on a constrained connection. Fix a failed essential action or unreadable restriction before release; document a cosmetic exception with an owner rather than assuming another client's preview resolves it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Keep the offer, links and recipient permissions in sync</h2>
      <p>Give each release a template version and an offer record covering products, eligibility, price basis, currency, expiry timezone and stacking rules. Reconcile the subject, artwork, live text, plain-text version, landing page and checkout against that record. Follow every final tracked link, including redirects, to the intended product, variant, locale and offer. Test rejection for an ineligible customer as well as acceptance for an eligible one.</p>
      <p>Recheck channel-specific permission, suppression and relevant journey exits when selecting the audience and again at the sending boundary supported by the system. An email layout approval is not permission to text the same people. A purchase can make a cart reminder obsolete; an email unsubscribe should update its intended scope without silently manufacturing permission in another channel. Set timing and contact rules for the markets and message categories involved with qualified advice where needed.</p>
      <p>Keep sender identity, contact information and the applicable unsubscribe or preference controls legible and usable. Test with a controlled recipient, follow the opt-out path, then attempt a later promotional selection to confirm the intended suppression. Check how copied templates and migrated profiles retain footer bindings and suppression state. A visible footer alone does not establish legal compliance. Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> to frame the operational review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Approve a release and prepare a bounded recovery</h2>
      <p>Assign separate owners for copy and offer truth, data mapping, visual review and final sending. Record approvals against the same immutable template, assets, audience rule and offer version. Any change to a destination, restriction, personalization rule or shared module should reopen the affected checks. Limit editing and sending privileges to the roles that need them.</p>
      <p>Keep a known-good template and a change log. Before replacing a shared module, identify scheduled campaigns and active flows that reference it; test a copy and decide which dependents should migrate. Retire obsolete versions so a later campaign cannot accidentally reuse an expired offer. Keep suppression history separate from template cleanup.</p>
      <p>If a bad version escapes, pause queued campaigns and affected flow steps, identify recipients and versions already sent, and correct controlled destinations where appropriate. Reverting a template does not recall delivered email. Decide whether a correction is useful, authorized and accurate before sending one; avoid duplicate resends to everyone. Resume after the failed fixture passes, with an owner monitoring the next release.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These are hypothetical test cases to adapt to a store's messages, not merchant results or claims that every email client behaves alike. Use synthetic recipients, record the sent version and keep the expected result beside the observed result.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Proposed acceptance condition</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Images blocked; linked hero unavailable</td><td>Purpose, terms and main text action remain understandable; the functional image alternative identifies its destination.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Missing name and empty recommendation feed</td><td>Neutral greeting and approved omitted module appear; no raw tokens, broken image or unrelated customer data.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Long translated product and enlarged text on narrow screen</td><td>Reading order stays meaningful; full labels and terms remain reachable without obscuring the action.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dark appearance with transparent logo</td><td>Identity, body text and action remain distinguishable in each recorded target client; failures block that release or require an explicit approved remedy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Eligible offer, expired offer and wrong-currency destination</td><td>Eligible checkout matches approved terms; invalid cases reject accurately or explain the current state without promising the expired benefit.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email opt-out after preview; SMS-only permission</td><td>Promotional email selection excludes the opted-out profile and does not treat SMS-only permission as email permission.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purchase after cart-email scheduling</td><td>The configured purchase exit prevents the obsolete reminder; delayed event handling is surfaced for resolution.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shared-module edit, stale feed and rollback</td><td>Affected dependents are identified, required data failure blocks the offer, and restoring the approved version does not duplicate already-sent messages.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Choose a workflow that the team can operate</h2>
      <p>A unified email and SMS workspace can fit when one team owns shared audience rules and can demonstrate channel-specific permissions and synchronized offers. Separate senders can fit when each channel needs its own workflow and a named operator maintains event mapping, suppression and collision checks. An email-led workflow can fit product education when the message needs explanation and imagery; SMS should have a distinct, permissioned purpose rather than compensate for an unreadable email.</p>
      <p>Evaluate editors with your own fixtures: reusable modules, data fallbacks, translation review, final-message previews, role permissions, version history and export or recovery paths. Verify current capabilities and plan restrictions directly with a shortlisted supplier. Use <a class="link" href="/compare">app comparisons</a> and the <a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives directory</a> to organize the decision around those requirements.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Measure comprehension and completed decisions</h2>
      <p>Track rendering defects by client and template version, broken destinations, fallback frequency, approval rework and support contacts about confusing terms. Define denominators: broken-link reports per delivered message and fallback uses per rendered personalized message answer different questions. Review unsubscribes and complaints alongside purchases and contribution after refunds, incentive effects and sending costs.</p>
      <p>For a design experiment, randomly assign eligible recipients to stable variants and keep audience, offer, timing and landing page consistent. Change one meaningful design decision, such as the order of explanation and product details. Predefine the observation window, primary outcome and harm thresholds. Compare completed actions per assigned eligible recipient, including people who did not click, and report uncertainty; clicks or opens alone do not establish commercial lift.</p>
      <p>For example, in a hypothetical experiment, 24 completed actions among 1,000 assigned recipients is 2.4%, while 20 among 1,000 is 2.0%: a difference of 0.4 percentage points, not proof of a reliable improvement. Allow for delayed purchases, returns and other channel exposure. A no-send holdout addresses the effect of sending; a comparison between two designs addresses their difference. See the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics playbook</a> for the broader measurement contract.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
